pull_request.body:
Fixes #1018. This takes over #1021 by @maximebuyse, whose tests showed two cases where Lean reports `don't know how to synthesize implicit argument`. They have different causes, so this PR fixes them in separate commits.

### Constructors

A constructor whose type parameters aren't determined by its arguments was extracted without its type, so Lean couldn't elaborate it, even when the Rust annotates it. For instance

```rust
let x: Option<u32> = None;
assert!(x.is_none());
```

extracted to `core.option.Option.is_none none`, and Lean couldn't infer `T`. `Ok(2)` hits the same error for the error type.

`add_type_annotations_to_fun_decl` never annotated constructors. For Lean, this PR:

- annotates a constructor or a structure literal when one of its type or const generic parameters is neither known from the context nor determined by a field whose type Lean can infer by itself. A tuple counts as inferable only if its components are;
- counts a call's implicit parameter as known when another argument determines it, so `mem::replace(x, None)` or the end of a list literal get no annotation;
- sends nullary constructors like `None` through the same logic. The head of an application is no longer visited when it is a qualified identifier, which was returned unchanged anyway, so constructors don't loop.

### Tuple structs

A tuple struct is extracted as a type definition: `Unit` if it has no fields, the type of its field if it has one, and the product of the types of its fields otherwise. This definition drops the parameters that the fields don't use. `compute_explicit_info` still counted them as determined by an input of that type, so they were implicit. This is the E3 case of #1021:

```rust
pub struct Wrap<const N: usize>;

pub fn use_wrap<const N: usize>(_w: &Wrap<N>) -> bool { true }

pub fn test_const_generic() -> bool {
    let w: Wrap<4> = Wrap;
    use_wrap(&w)
}
```

extracted to `def use_wrap {N : Std.Usize} (_w : Wrap N)` and `use_wrap ()`, and Lean couldn't infer `N`. Annotating the argument doesn't help, since `(() : Wrap 4#usize)` still has type `Unit`. This PR:

- adds `TypesUtils.tuple_struct_params_in_fields`, which gives the parameters of a tuple struct that appear in its fields. It looks through the tuple structs that the fields use (`PhantomData` is a unit struct too);
- makes `compute_explicit_info` skip, in the inputs, the parameters that a tuple struct drops. `use_wrap` now takes `N` explicitly, and the call is `use_wrap 4#usize ()`. The trait clauses are unchanged, since the type of an instance keeps the parameters;
- uses this for the signatures of functions, trait methods and loops. `translate_fun_sig_from_decomposed` now takes the `decls_ctx` for this.

### Tests

Tests are `#[verify::test]` functions:

- `tests/src/constructor-type-annotations.rs`: `None`, `Ok`, `Err`, `Some(None)`, a user-defined enum, and `None` inside a tuple and inside a structure (shapes from the tests of #1021). All seven fail to elaborate without the change. `Some(None)` gets a single annotation, on the outer `Some`;
- `tests/src/tuple-struct-params.rs`: a unit struct, a tuple struct with one field, one whose parameter only appears in a nested `PhantomData`, and a loop that carries a unit struct. All four fail to elaborate without the change. Two more check that a parameter stays implicit when a field uses it or another input determines it;
- `tests/src/options.rs`: the `Result::unwrap_or` tests now use an annotated `Ok(2)` and `Err(3)` directly, instead of the `ok_if_even` helper that #1380 needed because of this bug. Without the change, the `Ok` test fails to elaborate.

The tests of #1021 (`issue_1018.rs` and `issue_1018_edge.rs`) pass as they are.

Among the existing outputs, regenerating all tests with `IN_CI=1` only changes `Options.lean`, for the `unwrap_or` tests. The closure structs that are extracted as `Unit` keep their implicit parameters, because a trait clause determines them.

